── THE LIMIT, REPORTED EVERY RUN RATHER THAN DISCOVERED ──
This compares a picker against the CHECK IN sql/, which is the
repo's INTENDED constraint and not the live database's. A role
that is in the schema file and NOT YET MIGRATED is invisible here
-- that is the office_manager state as this is written. So:
  CAUGHT    a picker ahead of the schema file (drift)
  NOT CAUGHT a schema file ahead of production (lag)
  NOT CAUGHT a picker value that is in NEITHER the app's
             ROLES_BY_APP vocabulary NOR the CHECK -- a
             MISSPELT role is invisible here. Closing it means
             deciding which non-role option values are legitimate
             per app, which is a vocabulary nobody has written.

2 CHECK-widening migration(s) found. Each is a lag this check cannot measure:
  * sairncare: 2026-10-14-michael-run-order.sql widens the CHECK by ALTER, so the repo allows [owner, nursing, med_aide, caregiver, billing, activities, office_manager] and PRODUCTION allows that only if the file has been run
  * sairnlegacy: 2026-10-14-michael-run-order.sql widens the CHECK by ALTER, so the repo allows [owner, director, arranger, prep, office, apprentice, livery, staff] and PRODUCTION allows that only if the file has been run
  The live question is one query per app:
    select pg_get_constraintdef(oid) from pg_constraint
     where conname = '<app>_employee_auth_role_check';

